    "topic_path": "History, Globalization, and Values-Based Leadership/Ancient History: Management Through the 1990s/Fayol’s 14 Principles of Management",
    "content": "2. Authority/Responsibility Managers must have the authority to issue commands, but with that authority comes the responsibility to ensure that the work gets done.\n\n4. Unity of Command An employee should receive orders only from one boss to avoid conflicting instructions.\n\n5. Unity of Direction Each unit or group has only one boss and follows one plan so that work is coordinated.\n\n9. Line of Authority The line of authority moves from top management down to the lowest ranks. This hierarchy is necessary for unity of command, but communication can also occur laterally if the bosses are kept aware of it. The line should not be overextended or have too many levels.\n\n14. Esprit de Corps Harmony and team spirit across the organization builds morale and unity.",
